eau3 aPLA's logistics on the Tibetan Plateau 1aMontgomery, AL :bChina Aerospace Studies Institute,c2025.  a1 online resource (57 pages) :bcolor illustrations, color maps  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  atextbPDF2rdaft  a"November 2025."  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  aIncludes bibliographical references (pages 43-57).0 aIntroduction -- PLA force structure and logistical support in Tibet -- Remote basing: Logistical support methods.  a"The People's Liberation Army (PLA) is at the forefront of China's efforts to defend its extensive land border with India and maintain firm control over Tibet. As both China and India have expanded their respective infrastructural and military basing networks along the Line of Actual Control (the sectors of the Sino-Indian border where demarcation remains disputed), significant escalations have occurred, most notably the Doklam Standoff in mid-2017 and the Galwan Valley skirmish in June 2020. For the PLA, providing logistical support and sustainment to troops stationed on the "roof of the world" is a longstanding challenge. Nevertheless, despite the generally difficult geography on the Chinese side of the border, more effective logistical support appears to remain an enduring advantage for the PLA over the Indian Armed Forces."--Publisher's description.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover (airuniversity.af.edu, viewed April 16, 2026).10aChina.bZhongguo ren min jie fang junxProcurement. 0aMilitary baseszTibetan Plateau. 0aChinaxForeign relationszIndia. 0aIndiaxForeign relationszChina. 6aChine0(CaQQLa)201-0053864xRelations extérieures0(CaQQLa)201-0053864zInde.0(CaQQLa)201-0482676 6aInde0(CaQQLa)201-0