in Winter of Wyoming in the House of Representatives, Wednesday, February 17, 1926. 1aWashington :bUnited States Government Printing Office,c1926.  a29 pages ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP); no FDLP item number available.  aDescription based on online version of print resource (GovInfo, viewed Aug. 6, 2026); title from title page.  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds. 0aPublic landszUnited States.1 aUnited States.bCongress.bHouse.  aC0bGPO  abc34 20260806b20260806  aVEND01b2026082601607cam a2200337 i 001477565OCoLC20261014171356.0260806s1928    mau          f000 0 eng d  a(OCoLC)1610468250  aGPObengerdacGPOdOCLCOdGPO  an-us---  aGPOO0 aY 1.2:W 73/31 aWinter, Charles E.q(Charles Edwin),d1870-1948,eauthor.1https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/entity/E39PBJd8bhRVKpHvHc3KhcKw4q10aWashington and Lincoln : speech of Hon. Charles E. Winter of Wyoming in the House of Representatives.3 aWashington and Lincoln: speech of Honorable Charles Edwin Winter of Wyoming in the House of Representatives, Wednesday, February 25, 1929. 1aWashington :bUnited States Government Printing Office,c1926.  a8 pages ;c24 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  aunmediatedbn2rdamedia  avolumebnc2rdacarrier  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP); no FDLP item number available  aDescription based on online version of print resource (GovInfo, viewed Aug. 6, 2026); title from title page.  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.10aWashington, George,d1732-1799.10aLincoln, Abraham,d1809-1865.1 aUnited States.bCongress.bHouse.  aC0bGPO  abc34 20260806b20260806  aVEND01b2026082603671cam a2200505 i 001477569OCoLC20261014165942.0m     o  d s      cr |||||||||||260423s2026    dcu     ob   s000 0 eng c  a(OCoLC)1586934463  aMLRbengerdacMLRdOCLCOdGPOdOCLCOdOCLCFdOCLCOdGPO  apcc  an-us-mn  aGPOO 4aHV6698.M6bC67 2026  a1016-C (online)0 aY 4.G 74/7:C 82/1604aThe Cost of Doing Nothing :bHow Tim Walz and Keith Ellison Fueled Minnesota's Fraud Explosion. 1a[Washington, D.C.] :bHouse Committee on Oversight and Government Reform,c2026.  a1 online resource (53 pages)  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  a"March 4, 2026."  a"An Interim Staff Report by the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform Majority Staff."  a"In December 2025, the House Committee on Oversight and Government Reform launched an investigation into fraud in Minnesota's social services programs that occurred under Governor Tim Walz and Attorney General Keith Ellison's watch. The Committee's investigation has found that senior officials in Minnesota state government, including Governor Walz and Attorney General Ellison, were aware of widespread fraud in federally funded social services programs for years, possessed the legal and procedural authority to stop payments, but repeatedly failed to act. As a result, potentially billions of American taxpayer dollars were allowed to flow to fraudulent actors, while vulnerable populations were harmed and whistleblowers were ignored, sidelined, and retaliated against. While the Committee's investigation remains ongoing, testimony and documents obtained to date establish a consistent pattern: fraud warnings were elevated to senior levels of the Minnesota state government, meaningful corrective action was delayed or avoided, and payments continued long after credible red flags emerged. The Committee's findings raise serious questions about governance and oversight in Minnesota's administration of federal funds. The failure to act decisively in the face of known fraud allowed criminal schemes to flourish and diverted resources away from eligible recipients: the vulnerable populations these programs were intended to serve."--Page 2.  aIn scope of the U.S. Government Publishing Office Cataloging and Indexing Program (C&I) and Federal Depository Library Program (FDLP).  aThis is a publication issued by the United States federal government or produced with federal funds.  aDescription based on online resource; title from PDF cover (viewed April 23, 2026).10aWalz, Tim,d1964-10aEllison, Keith,d1963-10aMinnesota.bAttorney General. 0aLegislative oversightzUnited States. 0aFraudzMinnesota. 0aExecutive departmentszMinnesota. 0aAdministrative agencieszMinnesota.1 aUnited States.bCongress.bHouse.bCommittee on Oversight and Government Reform,eauthor,eissuing body.40uhttps://purl.fdlp.gov/GPO/gpo258817704 zAddress at time of PURL creationuhttps://oversight.house.gov/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/The-Cost-of-Doing-Nothing_How-Tim-Walz-and-Keith-Ellison-Fueled-Minnesotas-Fraud-Explosion_3.4.26_FINAL.pdf  aC0bGPO  abc34 20260806b20260806  aBIBCONEWb20260806  aVEND01b2026082601935cam a2200385 a 00147759620261013102429.0he bmb   bbca820805s1978    lau     bc   f000 0 eng d  a43949017  a(OCoLC)8659513z(OCoLC)43949017  aAFUbengcAFUdOCLdUPMdOCLCQdOCLCFdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCQdOCLCOdOCLCQdGPO  aGPOO  a0022-A-09 (MF)0 aA 106.19:OK 600aCatalog of seed and vegetative stock available from the Southern Regional Plant Introduction Station :bokra (Abelmoschus esculentus) /ccomplied by Regional Project S-9 of the Agricultural Experiment Stations of Alabama [and others].30aOkra (Abelmoschus esculentus)  aNew Orleans, La. :bAgricultural Research (Southern Region), Science and Education Administration, U.S. Dept. of Agriculture,c1978.  avi, 7 pages ;c26 cm  atextbtxt2rdacontent  ami